WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:08.217
Regularization adds an explicit parameter preference to an objective. Normalization changes how intermediate values are scaled,

00:00:08.217 --> 00:00:16.234
while stable numerical formulas prevent avoidable overflow, underflow and cancellation. These mechanisms can interact, but one

00:00:16.234 --> 00:00:19.240
does not substitute for the others.

00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:27.427
The one-half factor is part of our definition. Check it before comparing regularization strengths across implementations.

00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:33.756
This term pulls a positive weight toward zero. It is a modelling preference, not a measured

00:00:33.756 --> 00:00:34.483
prediction residual.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:40.816
Which part of the update changes when only the penalty coefficient changes? Pause here and make

00:00:40.816 --> 00:00:42.270
a prediction before continuing.

00:00:43.400 --> 00:00:52.547
Both terms contribute to ordinary gradient descent on the regularized objective. For our starting inputs, Data

00:00:52.547 --> 00:00:57.120
gradient: 0.5. Penalty gradient: 0.4. Updated weight: 1.91.

00:00:57.633 --> 00:01:04.207
For this simple SGD update the penalty gives a multiplicative shrinkage. Decoupled weight decay with an

00:01:04.207 --> 00:01:07.083
adaptive optimizer is a different update rule.

00:01:07.600 --> 00:01:14.470
Keep train/evaluation modes, loss normalization and optimizer conventions visible when reproducing a method.

